This is the classic exercism "connect" (Hex) exercise. The board is a parallelogram where row `i` has `i` leading spaces (which get stripped). After stripping, row `i` has length `n - i`? Let's check: the first row is ". . . ." (4 cells), the second row is " O X X X" → after stripping "OXXX" (4 cells), and so on. Actually, in these test cases, after stripping, all rows end up being the same length. Wait: "X can win on a 1x1 board" is a single "X".

The standard approach: treat the board as a parallelogram. Cell (row, col) has 6 neighbors:
- (row-1, col-1), (row-1, col) — the upper-left and upper-right neighbors
- (row, col-1), (row, col+1) — left and right
- (row+1, col-1), (row+1, col) — the lower-left and lower-right neighbors

Player X wins by connecting the left edge (col 0) to the right edge (col n-1). Player O wins by connecting the top edge (row 0) to the bottom edge (row n-1).

Note: after stripping spaces, the rows of a parallelogram have equal length (because the leading spaces are just for visual offset). Actually, wait — in the spec's example, the rows have 5 cells and the leading spaces increase. After stripping, all rows are 5 cells. So the board is a square n×n grid where the neighbors are as above (parallelogram topology).

