func connects(lines []string, player rune, horizontal bool) bool {
	rows := len(lines)
	cols := len(lines[0])
	visited := make([][]bool, rows)
	for i := range visited {
		visited[i] = make([]bool, cols)
	}

	var queue [][2]int
	for i := 0; i < rows; i++ {
		for j := 0; j < cols; j++ {
			start := horizontal && j == 0 || !horizontal && i == 0
			if start && rune(lines[i][j]) == player {
				queue = append(queue, [2]int{i, j})
				visited[i][j] = true
			}
		}
	}

	for len(queue) > 0 {
		r, c := queue[0][0], queue[0][1]
		queue = queue[1:]
		if horizontal && c == cols-1 || !horizontal && r == rows-1 {
			return true
		}
		for _, n := range neighbors(r, c, rows, cols) {
			nr, nc := n[0], n[1]
			if nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols || visited[nr][nc] {
				continue
			}
			if rune(lines[nr][nc]) != player {
				continue
			}
			visited[nr][nc] = true
			queue = append(queue, [2]int{nr, nc})
		}
	}
	return false
}
```

Wait, there's a subtle point: when we start BFS from all cells on the start edge, we should check whether the starting cell itself is on the end edge (a 1x1 board). The check inside the loop handles that: when we pop a starting cell, we check whether it's on the end edge. For a 1x1 "X": the starting cell (0,0) is popped, c == cols-1 == 0 → true. Good.

But note: if horizontal, the starting cell is on the left edge; if cols == 1, it's also on the right edge → immediate win. That's correct.

But there's a subtle bug: when we pop a cell, we check the end edge before expanding. The starting cells are all on the start edge; if the board is 1 cell wide, the start edge == end edge. No problem.

Also, regarding the end-edge check: a cell popped from the queue that is on the end edge means the path reached the end edge. Correct.

